 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of stages $D$ in the dynamic growth formulation, as it sums losses from all active outlets [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storing intermediate outputs and uncertainty maps for all active stages during the backward pass, increasing memory usage compared to single-stage losses [S1].
-*   **Strengths**: Effectively balances global structure and local detail, preventing the "blurring" effect common in single-scale optimizations [S2]. Adapts to network growth dynamically [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: Requires careful tuning of multiple hyperparameters ($\alpha, \beta_1, \beta_2$). The uncertainty loss term may be sensitive to the quality of the uncertainty estimation [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of stages $D$, as each stage's output is rendered and loss-computed [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing intermediate outputs for all $D$ stages during the forward pass to compute gradients [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Prevents overfitting to high-frequency noise by enforcing low-frequency consistency; improves detail sharpness in sparse-view settings [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of weights ($\beta, \alpha$); dynamic growth adds complexity to the training loop [S1, S2].
+*   **When to use:** When training radiance fields with dynamic growth (Recursive-NeRF) or when refining denoised inputs for sparse-view reconstruction (Denoise-GS) [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of multi-scale or joint loss functions in radiance field optimization emerged to address the limitations of single-resolution training. The dynamic growth approach with multi-stage loss was introduced in **Recursive-NeRF** [S1], which dynamically grows the network depth based on uncertainty clustering. The coarse-fine joint loss formulation was utilized in **Denoise-GS** [S2] for self-supervised denoising in sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting, aiming to refine denoised images while preserving structural consistency.
+The concept originates from two distinct lines of work in differentiable volume rendering and Gaussian Splatting. The multi-stage summation formulation was introduced in **Recursive-NeRF** (2022) by [S1], which uses a dynamically growing network where losses are aggregated across all active sub-networks. The coarse-fine balance formulation was introduced in **Denoise-GS** (2026) by [S2], applied to self-supervised denoising for sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting to preserve structural consistency while enhancing texture fidelity.
 
